n about  extensibility so we're really leveraging  that to make sure the project stays at a  reasonable size the community  contributors now are dealing with issues  of compatibility and growth and scope  like every new change has a chance to  break every one of these users and so we  have to be more careful than we've ever  been  and  one of your talks this week was about CR  DS and that's one of the main mechanisms  for extensibility can we deep dive into  that a little bit and get technical what  are some of the challenges with CR DS  why are we why do they exist how should  people use them are they only for  external people are they part of the  core project what how does that work  thanks for the lead in this was my  lightning talk this week it was really  about the birth of CR DS custom resource  definitions this is our one of our  extensibility mechanisms and how that's  being used in kubernetes and it was one  of those things that was summarizing and  emergent you know it's one of these  things if you set out to do on purpose  you probably would fail at but because  it emerged organically from the project  it was really successful it's a way that  people are extending kubernetes to add  their own types their own concepts into  our API machinery it started off as a  thing for people to just prototype and  play with and it wasn't really very good  it was very second class but now it's  very much a first-class thing we've been  adding more more support for versioning  and schema and validation to the point  now that you can actually build real  things with it and I know you know this  because we made you and your cig be one  of our pioneers all right and you guys  thanks for falling on that grenade but  like some of the storage work is  happening as CR DS it's not just an  extension it's actually built in as part  of the system it's just a resource  definition that's not encode it's in  declaration so what does that mean for  the built-in types are there going 