s and would be contributors  here 500 on the wait list yet and more  on the wait list they were here to talk  about how to contribute to kubernetes  what the state of the project is so I  think we need to make sure that we we  are doing things to make these people  successful they are the way that we were  able to grow sort of at the rocket ship  growth that we've been having and what  are some of those projects that we're  doing to help continue to sustain that  growth I mean we've got everything from  better documentation and onboarding we  had a new contributor track at the  contributor summit so this is for people  who are just starting out with  kubernetes to come and learn how does  our codebase work how does our how do  our bots all of the github workflow that  we've built how do those things work  we've also got people who are building  those bots we've got a group of people  we call inch prod at Google and they're  building these things like robots that  make interacting with github a little  smoother a little easier and scaling up  a little bit better just among things  we've also got all the documentation  people which is serving purposes both  for end users and for new contributors  in terms of leadership in the community  what I've noticed as I talked to ash and  Janet and other folks is that this  community really kind of incubates new  leaders is that natural is that  that was intended and how does that  happen and what are your thoughts on  that  I take it as a real compliment actually  it's very intentional  we know that projects only survive as  long as their leadership survives and I  know that I won't be here forever I want  this project to be around a long time so  I want to make sure that there's people  who know what they're doing who have  some real battle scars who know where  the bodies are buried who are coming up  through the ranks to to take over the  project I think this is a really  important part of the way the steering  committee is 